##DESCRIPTION
##  Mental Math problems using compensation.
##ENDDESCRIPTION

##KEYWORDS('mental math', 'subtraction')

## DBsubject('Arithmetic')
## DBchapter('Integers')
## DBsection('Mental Math')
## Author('Peter Staab')
## Institution('Fitchburg State University')
## Date('5/3/2020')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

# The following prevents answers from containing operations.
Context('LimitedNumeric');

$k1   = non_zero_random(-5, 5);
$a1   = random(2, 4) * 100 + $k1;
$a2   = random(3, 5) * 100 + random(40, 60);
$ans1 = Compute($a1 + $a2);

$k2 = non_zero_random(-5, 5);
$b2 = random(3, 5) * 100 + random(40, 60);
$b1 = random(2, 4) * 100 + $k2;

$k3 = non_zero_random(-2, 2);
$c1 = random(2,  4) * 10 + $k3;
$c2 = random(50, 80);

$d1   = 4 * random(11, 17);
$ans4 = Compute($d1 * 25);

$e1 = random(20, 25);
$e2 = 5 * $e1;

BEGIN_PGML

Answer the following addition problems using the mental math technique compensation:

a. [`[$a1]+[$a2]=\;`] [__]{Compute($a1+$a2)}
b. [`[$b2]-[$b1]=\;`] [__]{Compute($b2-$b1)}
c. [`[$c2]-[$c1]=\;`] [__]{Compute($c2-$c1)}
d. [`[$d1]\times 25=\;`] [__]{Compute(25*$d1)}
e. [`[$e2]\div 5=\;`] [__]{Compute($e1)}

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
a. In this case, note that the first addend is close to a multiple of 100. 
[```[$a1]+[$a2] = ([$a1]-[$k1]) + ([$a2]+[$k1]) = [$a1-$k1] + [$a2+$k1] = [$a1+$a2]```]
b. The subtrahend (2nd number) of the subtraction is close to a multiple of 100. 
[```[$b2]-[$b1] = ([$b2]-[$k2])-([$b1]-[$k2]) = [$b2-$k2]-[$b1-$k2] = [$b2-$b1]```]
c. The subtrahend is close to a multiple of 10.
[``` [$c2]-[$c1] = ([$c2]-[$k3])-([$c1]-[$k3]) = [$c2-$k3]-[$c1-$k3] = [$c2-$c1] ```]
d. Multiplying by 25 can be done by dividing by 4, then multiplying by 100. 
[``` [$d1] \times 25 = [$d1] \div 4 \times 100 = [$d1/4] \times 100 = [$d1*25] ```]
e. Dividing by 5 can be done by multiplying by 2 then dividing by 10.
[```[$e2] \div 5 = [$e2] \times 2 \div 10 = [$e2*2] \div 10 = [$e1]```]
END_PGML_SOLUTION
ENDDOCUMENT();
